<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Iran–Iraq War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1980">1980</a>–<a href="/years/1988">1988</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>–Iraq War was fought from September 1980 to August 1988 after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saddam-hussein">Saddam Hussein</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> invaded Iran, seeking control of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shatt-al-arab">Shatt al-Arab</a> and hoping to exploit the turmoil of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iranian-revolution">Iranian Revolution</a>. After early Iraqi gains, Iran recovered its territory by 1982 and the war became a costly stalemate of trench warfare, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chemical-weapons">chemical weapons</a> and attacks on cities and oil tankers. It ended in a UN-brokered ceasefire without change of borders, with hundreds of thousands dead.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/iran-iraq-war.jpg" alt="Iran–Iraq War" width="330" height="227" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mahmoud Badrfar · GFDL · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chemical_weapon1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The two states had long disputed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shatt-al-arab">Shatt al-Arab</a>. In 1937 they agreed that Iranian ships would pay tolls on the river, and in 1969 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> stopped paying. Tension led to bloodshed until the 1975 Algiers Agreement, in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> ceded the eastern bank to Iran. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iranian-revolution">Iranian Revolution</a> of 1979, Iraq feared that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruhollah-khomeini">Ruhollah Khomeini</a> would export Iran's new ideology and rally Iraq's Shia majority against its Sunni Muslim-controlled Baʽathist government, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saddam-hussein">Saddam Hussein</a> also wished to replace Iran as the main power in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a>. Iran's army was weakened: by September 1980 about 12,000 officers had been purged. On 10 September Iraq took territories at Zain al-Qaws and Saif Saad that Iran had not handed over under the 1975 agreement.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> invaded on 22 September 1980 with air strikes on ten Iranian airfields, which failed to destroy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>'s air force. The battle for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorramshahr">Khorramshahr</a> left some 7,000 dead on each side and delayed the Iraqis enough for Iran to deploy its forces. The advance had stalled by December, and the war settled into trench warfare. Iran recaptured all its territory by June 1982 and then invaded Iraq. For the next five years Iraq stayed mostly on the defensive while Iran launched more than 70 offensives, often by human wave assaults.</p><p>The war also reached shipping and cities. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> bombed Iranian tankers, economic targets and cities, and from February 1988 launched over 200 al-Husayn missiles at 37 Iranian cities. It used <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chemical-weapons">chemical weapons</a>, and in mid-1988 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poison-gas">poison gas</a> bombing of the town of Oshnavieh killed and wounded over 2,000 civilians. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and most Arab states supported Iraq, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a> and others supported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> accepted a ceasefire under United Nations Security Council Resolution 598, effective in August 1988. The fear of chemical attack on its civilians weighed heavily on its leadership. The war caused around 500,000 deaths, not counting the related Anfal campaign. It brought no permanent change of borders, and neither country received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-reparations">reparations</a>. Both sides suffered financially, and low-level conflict continued until relations improved after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saddam-hussein">Saddam Hussein</a> was overthrown in 2003.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Iran–Iraq War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Saudi Arabia and Kuwait spent $25 billion backing Saddam Hussein in the <b>Iran–Iraq War</b> of 1980–1988, yet after Iraq's invasion of Kuwait in 1990 King Fahd invited American and coalition troops onto Saudi soil, and in 1991 Saudi forces flew bombing raids and joined the land invasion that…</blockquote><a href="/empire/saudi-arabia">Saudi Arabia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The movement’s seventh summit was to meet in Baghdad in 1982, during the <b>Iran–Iraq War</b>; after Iranian aircraft bombed Baghdad’s Al-Dura refinery to show that its airspace was unsafe, the summit was held in New Delhi in March 1983.</blockquote><a href="/movement/non-aligned-movement">Non-Aligned Movement · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Its melody nevertheless continued to circulate and served as a morale-boosting symbol during the <b>Iran–Iraq War</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/ey-iran">Ey Iran</a></li></ul></section>
